// Preload: what the language itself refers to, written in Rig. Every
// program imports it without an #import (spec 2.14): an ordinary program,
// a build program and each of its workspaces, rig check, a program the test
// mode runs interpreted (unit-tests --interpret), and the world of #run
// while compiling. rig finds it where it finds every module that ships with
// it, and reads this file right after the program's main file. In load
// order it comes first, before the main file (spec 2.13), so its
// declarations come first: a program that declares a name Preload declares
// is told so at its own declaration. Writing
//
//     #import "Preload";
//
// is allowed and reads nothing more, just as a #load of this file by its
// path reads nothing more: a file is read once.
//
// It holds the allocator's shape and the heap, type information, Any, the
// context, the temporary storage and its allocator, [..]T's layout and the
// helpers of its built-ins, Resizable_Array, Source_Code_Location (the
// place a default #caller_location is), and the command-line arguments.
//
// What Preload declares joins the program's one top level, so a program
// can't declare those names. README.md lists the ones that are for
// programs. Every name Preload declares for its own use starts with two
// underscores (__malloc, say), which leaves the plain names to programs:
// a convention, which the compiler doesn't enforce.

// The allocator: where memory comes from. Whatever needs memory takes an
// allocator, so the caller decides where it comes from and how it goes
// back; left out, it is the context's, context.allocator (below). Preload
// holds its shape, its modes and the heap, which every program has; Basic
// adds the helpers (alloc, resize, free and free_all), an arena, a pool,
// and the temporary storage's own helpers, talloc, its marks and its reset.
//
// An allocator is Jai's shape: one procedure and the data it works on. The
// procedure is called with a mode, ALLOCATE, RESIZE, FREE or FREE_ALL, and
// does what the mode says, with the data as its last argument: an arena's
// procedure gets its Arena, a pool's its Pool, the heap's nothing. Anything
// that holds an Allocator can be given any of them, or one a program writes
// itself, without knowing which.

// The modes an allocator procedure is called with (constants until enums).
ALLOCATE :: 0;
RESIZE :: 1;
FREE :: 2;
FREE_ALL :: 3;

// Jai's shape: one procedure and its data. The procedure type is written
// out, since Rig has no type aliases yet.
//
// The procedure is called as proc(mode, size, old_size, old_memory, data):
//
// - ALLOCATE: size bytes, or null.
// - RESIZE: old_memory, which holds old_size bytes, made size bytes long,
//   moved or not, with the first of its old bytes kept; or null, and then
//   old_memory is as it was.
// - FREE: gives old_memory back. Returns null.
// - FREE_ALL: gives back everything the allocator gave. Returns null.
Allocator :: struct {
    proc: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void;
    data: *void;
}

// C's heap, malloc, realloc and free, declared under Preload's own names
// (#foreign libc "NAME", spec 7.6), so that a program keeps C's: it may
// declare C's malloc, realloc or free itself, and its free and __free are
// the same C function.
__malloc :: (size: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "malloc";
__realloc :: (memory: *void, size: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "realloc";
__free :: (memory: *void) #foreign libc "free";

// The heap: C's malloc, realloc and free, through Preload's own
// declarations. Its memory is aligned for any type, as C's is.
//
// FREE_ALL does nothing: the heap doesn't know what it gave, so each piece
// goes back with FREE. A size above 0 converts to C's size_t, a u64,
// unchanged. The procedure asks C for exactly what it's given: Basic's
// helpers never ask it for 0 bytes, whose result C leaves to each C
// library, nor give it null to resize or free.
heap_allocator_proc :: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void {
    if mode == ALLOCATE { return __malloc(cast(u64) size); }
    if mode == RESIZE { return __realloc(old_memory, cast(u64) size); }
    if mode == FREE { __free(old_memory); }
    return null;
}

// The heap as an Allocator. It needs no data.
HEAP_ALLOCATOR :: Allocator.{heap_allocator_proc, null};

// Type information (spec 3.16): Jai's, trimmed to Rig's types.
// type_info(T) (spec 5.19) gives a *Type_Info, the address of a
// description of T laid out as the structs below, and an Any pairs a
// value's address with its type's description. The compiler writes each
// description, once per type, in the program's read-only data (spec 10.3),
// and in the interpreter's memory while compiling (spec 10.8), laid out as
// these structs are: src/types.h mirrors them. A description is read-only:
// writing one is a memory error no check catches (spec 8.6).
//
// Every description starts with a Type_Info, whose tag says its kind. Each
// kind's struct holds that Type_Info as its first field, info (Rig has no
// using), so a *Type_Info whose tag says the kind is cast to the kind's
// struct: cast(*Type_Info_Struct) t when t.tag == TYPE_INFO_STRUCT.

// The kinds, a Type_Info's tag (constants until enums), numbered as Jai's
// tags are.
TYPE_INFO_INTEGER :: 0;
TYPE_INFO_FLOAT :: 1;
TYPE_INFO_BOOL :: 2;
TYPE_INFO_STRING :: 3;
TYPE_INFO_POINTER :: 4;
TYPE_INFO_PROCEDURE :: 5;
TYPE_INFO_VOID :: 6;
TYPE_INFO_STRUCT :: 7;
TYPE_INFO_ARRAY :: 8;

// What every description starts with: its kind, then the type's size and
// alignment in bytes (spec 3.11; void's are 0 and 1). A float, bool,
// string or void (TYPE_INFO_FLOAT, _BOOL, _STRING, _VOID) is described by
// this alone: a float's size says which it is.
Type_Info :: struct { tag: int; size: int; alignment: int; }

// TYPE_INFO_INTEGER: s8 to u64, and int. int and s64 are two types with
// two descriptions, the same in all but their addresses.
Type_Info_Integer :: struct { info: Type_Info; signed: bool; }

// TYPE_INFO_POINTER: *T, and the description of the T it points at
// (void's for a *void).
Type_Info_Pointer :: struct { info: Type_Info; pointer_to: *Type_Info; }

// TYPE_INFO_PROCEDURE: a procedure type, its parameters' types and its
// results', in order, whether it's called as C calls (#c_call), and whether
// its last parameter is a ..T, whose type is then described as []T.
Type_Info_Procedure :: struct {
    info: Type_Info;
    parameter_types: []*Type_Info;
    result_types: []*Type_Info;
    is_c_call: bool;
    is_variadic: bool;
}

// TYPE_INFO_STRUCT: a struct, by its declared name, and its fields in
// order, none for a struct with none. A struct that holds a pointer to
// itself is described by pointing back at its own description: the data is
// cyclic, not infinite.
Type_Info_Struct :: struct { info: Type_Info; name: string; members: []Type_Info_Struct_Member; }

// One field of a struct: its name, its type's description, and where it is
// in the struct, in bytes from its start (spec 3.9).
Type_Info_Struct_Member :: struct { name: string; type: *Type_Info; offset: int; }

// TYPE_INFO_ARRAY: an array, [N]T, a view, []T, or a resizable array, [..]T,
// which array_kind tells apart (ARRAY_FIXED, ARRAY_VIEW or ARRAY_RESIZABLE);
// the description of T; and N, or -1 for a view or a resizable array. A
// string is no view here: it has its own tag, TYPE_INFO_STRING.
Type_Info_Array :: struct { info: Type_Info; element_type: *Type_Info; array_kind: int; count: int; }

// A Type_Info_Array's array_kind, in Jai's order.
ARRAY_FIXED :: 0;
ARRAY_VIEW :: 1;
ARRAY_RESIZABLE :: 2;

// A value of any type: its type's description and its address. Every value
// converts to an Any implicitly (spec 3.16), a place by its own address, any
// other value by a temporary copy's, which lasts to the end of the
// statement; an Any converts as itself. It is an ordinary struct, so it has
// a description of its own, a struct named Any. The program reads the value
// by casting value_pointer once type says what it is.
Any :: struct { type: *Type_Info; value_pointer: *void; }

// The context (spec 7.10): what code deep in a call chain needs without
// every caller handing it down, Jai's way. Every Rig procedure but main and
// a #c_call one is given the caller's context, a *Context it isn't given in
// the source, and context names it; main starts with default_context.
// push_context runs a block with another (spec 6.13). It holds the
// allocator code takes its memory from when it's handed none, the
// temporary storage, and the place an allocation is asked from.

// A page of temporary storage, asked of the storage's overflow_allocator
// when the region before it, the first block or the newest page, can't
// hold an allocation: this header, then size bytes. The header is 24
// bytes, a multiple of 8, so the bytes after it are aligned to 8 when the
// page is. start is where its bytes start in the storage's count
// (Temporary_Storage.occupied, which counts from the first block's start),
// so the current page's own offset is occupied - start, found without
// walking the pages.
Temporary_Storage_Page :: struct { previous: *Temporary_Storage_Page; size: int; start: int; }

// Memory that is given out from one end and given back all at once:
// allocations from its first block, data's size bytes, then from pages
// asked of overflow_allocator, the newest first in overflow_pages. occupied
// is how far into the storage's count allocations reach, from the first
// block's start, counting the padding that aligns them and the unused
// tails of regions a page replaced; high_water_mark is the largest it has
// been.
Temporary_Storage :: struct {
    data: *u8;
    size: int;
    occupied: int;
    high_water_mark: int;
    overflow_allocator: Allocator;
    overflow_pages: *Temporary_Storage_Page;  // newest first
}

// The context: the allocator code takes its memory from when it's handed
// none, the temporary storage, and the place the allocation being asked for
// was asked from (spec 7.10). Basic's alloc, resize, free and free_all set
// allocation_location to their caller's place while they call the
// allocator, and put the one before back after, as the resizable arrays'
// helpers do with their built-in's place (below), so an allocator procedure
// can read where it is asked from, as a memory debugger records it, though
// Jai's shape of the procedure, which every allocator is written to, gives
// it no place. It is an ordinary field, which a program may set too:
// default_context starts with it zeroed, no place, each helper puts back
// the value it found, whatever that was, and a direct call of an
// allocator's procedure leaves it as it is, so the allocator reads whatever
// the context holds.
Context :: struct { allocator: Allocator; temporary_storage: *Temporary_Storage; allocation_location: Source_Code_Location; }

// The bytes of Preload's temporary storage's first block, and of the
// smallest page any storage asks for, its header included
// (temporary_allocator_proc).
TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE :: 32768;

// The context main and each #run start with, and the one a #c_call
// procedure, which C calls with none, pushes for itself when it has no
// other: the heap, Preload's temporary storage, and allocation_location
// zeroed, no place. __runtime_init sets it up.
default_context : Context;

// Preload's one temporary storage, which default_context holds, and its
// first block, as u64s so its allocations are 8-aligned.
__temporary_storage : Temporary_Storage;
__temporary_storage_data : [TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE / 8]u64;

// Sets default_context up, before main's first line and each #run's code:
// its allocator the heap, its temporary storage Preload's, whose first
// block is __temporary_storage_data. The pointers are set here, since a
// global's value can't hold another global's address (spec 4.3). Once: a
// second call does nothing. #c_call, so it uses no context itself.
__runtime_init :: () #c_call {
    if default_context.temporary_storage != null { return; }
    __temporary_storage.data = cast(*u8) *__temporary_storage_data[0];
    __temporary_storage.size = TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE;
    __temporary_storage.overflow_allocator = HEAP_ALLOCATOR;
    default_context.allocator = HEAP_ALLOCATOR;
    default_context.temporary_storage = *__temporary_storage;
}

// The program's command-line arguments (spec 7.4, 10.5): its name as it was
// started first, then each argument, each a string over the bytes of one of
// C's argv strings (on Windows, in a compiled program, over Preload's own
// UTF-8 copy of the C runtime's wide ones: __save_command_line), so a zero
// byte its count doesn't include follows it. main's prologue saves them,
// through __save_command_line, before main's first line; until then, and
// in the compile-time world, where no startup runs (spec 7.8), this is the
// empty view. Programs read it through Basic's get_command_line_arguments.
// Neither the elements nor the bytes are ever freed: they are the
// program's for the whole run.
__command_line_arguments : []string;

// What __save_command_line reads the arguments from on Windows (spec 10.6):
// the Universal C Runtime's _configure_wide_argv, which splits the command
// line into wide arguments too, its argc, argv and wide argv, and kernel32's
// WideCharToMultiByte, which converts UTF-16 to UTF-8. Under Preload's own
// names, as __malloc is, so that a program keeps C's and Windows' names: it
// may declare __p___argv itself. Every program imports their DLLs already,
// the C runtime's api-ms-win-crt-runtime set and KERNEL32.dll, so they cost
// it none more.
#if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
    __configure_wide_argv :: (mode: s32) -> s32 #foreign libc "_configure_wide_argv";
    __crt_argc :: () -> *s32 #foreign libc "__p___argc";
    __crt_argv :: () -> ***u8 #foreign libc "__p___argv";
    __crt_wargv :: () -> ***u16 #foreign libc "__p___wargv";
    __wide_char_to_multi_byte :: (code_page: u32, flags: u32, wide: *u16, wide_count: s32, bytes: *u8, byte_count: s32, default_char: *u8, used_default: *s32) -> s32 #foreign libc "WideCharToMultiByte";
}

// Saves C's argc and argv as __command_line_arguments. main's prologue
// calls it right after __runtime_init, with the argc and argv the C
// runtime gave C's main, which main takes though its Rig signature doesn't
// say so (spec 10.4). A null argv keeps what was saved: a Rig call of main
// passes 0 and null. An argc of 0 or less saves the empty view (Linux
// before 5.18 let a program be started with no argv[0], and a hand call
// may pass 0), except on Windows when argv is the C runtime's own (below).
// Otherwise the elements come from one __malloc, the i-th
// viewing argv[i]'s bytes up to its zero, which stay where C put them;
// when __malloc gives null, what was saved stays.
//
// On Windows the C runtime's argv isn't UTF-8: the C runtime converts the
// command line, which Windows keeps in UTF-16, to the ANSI code page, so
// Wörld's ö is one byte (F6 in code page 1252) and 日本 is ??, lost. So
// when argv is the C runtime's own, __argv, as a compiled main's prologue
// passes it, the arguments are the C runtime's wide ones instead, split by
// the same rules from the command line in UTF-16, each converted to UTF-8,
// an unpaired surrogate becoming U+FFFD (spec 10.6). The narrow argv was
// split from the line in the ANSI code page, so the two counts can differ:
// U+FF02, a fullwidth quote, is " in code page 1252. The arguments are as
// many as the wide count, which _configure_wide_argv puts in the C
// runtime's __argc; __argc then gets the narrow count back, so C code
// that reads it with __argv still finds them matching. A hand call that
// passes __argv gets the same, the argc it gives unused. Any other argv is
// saved as given: one a program or a #run makes by hand, and interpret
// mode's, which are UTF-8 already and never __argv (an interpreted
// program's __p___argv is ucrtbase.dll's own, whose __argv is null). When
// the wide arguments can't be had or converted, or __malloc gives null,
// what it took goes back and argv is saved as given. Issue #125's startup
// code may give main its argv in UTF-8 itself, from these same functions,
// so that argv is no longer __argv and this part is never taken: #125 may
// then delete it and its declarations.
//
// #c_call, so it uses no context, as __runtime_init; and, as it, it has no
// debug information, so a debugger steps over main's call of it. It is all
// one procedure, the Windows part too, so that none of it has any. Nothing
// it saves is ever freed.
__save_command_line :: (argc: s32, argv: **u8) #c_call {
    if argv == null { return; }
    #if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
        CP_UTF8 :: 65001;
        // _crt_argv_unexpanded_arguments: split, with no wildcard expanded,
        // as the narrow argv was.
        if argv == __crt_argv().* {
            // _configure_wide_argv sets __argc to the wide count, which is
            // read here; C code reads __argc with the narrow __argv, so it
            // gets the narrow count back whatever the call did.
            narrow_count := __crt_argc().*;
            configured := __configure_wide_argv(1) == 0;
            count := __crt_argc().*;
            __crt_argc().* = narrow_count;
            wide := __crt_wargv().*;
            // All the arguments' bytes in UTF-8, each one's zero included
            // (its size from WideCharToMultiByte given -1 counts it), or 0
            // when there are none or one can't be converted.
            total := 0;
            if configured && count > 0 && wide != null {
                for 0..count - 1 {
                    size := __wide_char_to_multi_byte(CP_UTF8, 0, wide[it], -1, null, 0, null, null);
                    if size <= 0 {
                        total = 0;
                        break;
                    }
                    total += size;
                }
            }
            if total > 0 {
                // One block for the elements, one for all the bytes, each
                // argument's right after the one before's zero. done
                // reaches total only when every one was converted. The
                // size is a u64's product, which a large count can't wrap.
                elements := cast(*string) __malloc(cast(u64) count * size_of(string));
                bytes := cast(*u8) __malloc(cast(u64) total);
                done := 0;
                if elements != null && bytes != null {
                    for 0..count - 1 {
                        size := __wide_char_to_multi_byte(CP_UTF8, 0, wide[it], -1, bytes + done, cast(s32) (total - done), null, null);
                        if size <= 0 {
                            done = -1;
                            break;
                        }
                        elements[it].data = bytes + done;
                        elements[it].count = size - 1;
                        done += size;
                    }
                }
                if done == total {
                    __command_line_arguments.data = elements;
                    __command_line_arguments.count = count;
                    return;
                }
                __free(elements);
                __free(bytes);
            }
        }
    }
    if argc <= 0 {
        empty : []string;
        __command_line_arguments = empty;
        return;
    }
    // A u64's product, as above, which a large argc can't wrap.
    elements := cast(*string) __malloc(cast(u64) argc * size_of(string));
    if elements == null { return; }
    for 0..argc - 1 {
        count := 0;
        while argv[it][count] != 0 { count += 1; }
        elements[it].data = argv[it];
        elements[it].count = count;
    }
    __command_line_arguments.data = elements;
    __command_line_arguments.count = argc;
}

// C's memmove, under Preload's own name, as __malloc is: the temporary
// allocator's RESIZE copies with it, and so do the resizable arrays'
// helpers that move or copy elements (__array_grow, __array_insert,
// __array_pop and the removes).
__memmove :: (destination: *void, source: *void, count: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "memmove";

// The temporary allocator's procedure. It works on the current context's
// temporary storage, context.temporary_storage, so data is unused: a
// push_context with another storage makes it allocate from that one. A
// program's own storage needs data, size and overflow_allocator set, and
// its data and overflow_allocator's memory aligned to 8, as the heap's is,
// for its allocations to be.
//
// It allocates from the current region, the newest page's bytes after its
// header or, with no page, the first block. The region's own offset is
// occupied - start (0 for the first block), since a page starts in the
// storage's count where the region before it ended, rounded up to a
// multiple of 8: so occupied keeps counting through the pages,
// high_water_mark is its largest value, and the newest page's offset is
// found without walking the pages. The rounding keeps a page's offsets as
// aligned as the count's, so the high water mark is at least the size of a
// first block that would have held everything.
//
// - ALLOCATE starts at the first multiple of 8 at or past the offset, so
//   every allocation is aligned to 8, as any Rig type needs (spec 3.11),
//   when the first block and overflow_allocator's memory are. When the
//   region's rest can't hold size bytes, a page is asked of
//   overflow_allocator, TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE bytes or size with the
//   header when that is larger, and the rest of the region before it goes
//   unused but counts as occupied. Its null is passed on, the storage as
//   it was, and a size whose page, with the header, would be past int's
//   largest gives null too, the storage as it was.
// - RESIZE grows or shrinks old_memory in place when it is the last
//   allocation and the region holds size bytes from it. Otherwise it
//   allocates anew and copies the old bytes that fit, or gives null with
//   old_memory as it was.
// - FREE does nothing. The memory goes back with everything else at the
//   next reset.
// - FREE_ALL is a reset: every page goes back to overflow_allocator, newest
//   first, and the first block is handed out again from its start. Memory
//   used after a reset is a memory error no check catches (spec 8.6). The
//   high water mark stays.
temporary_allocator_proc :: (mode: int, size: int, old_size: int, old_memory: *void, data: *void) -> *void {
    LARGEST :: 9223372036854775807; // int's largest value
    storage := context.temporary_storage;
    page := storage.overflow_pages;
    base := storage.data;
    region_size := storage.size;
    start := 0;
    if page != null {
        base = cast(*u8) page + size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page);
        region_size = page.size;
        start = page.start;
    }
    offset := storage.occupied - start;
    if mode == ALLOCATE {
        at := (offset + 7) & ~7;
        // Compared as size against what is left, rather than at + size
        // against the region's size, so that a size near int's largest
        // can't wrap the sum. at may be past the region's end, when offset
        // is within 7 of it, and then nothing is left.
        if size <= region_size - at {
            storage.occupied = start + at + size;
            if storage.occupied > storage.high_water_mark { storage.high_water_mark = storage.occupied; }
            return base + at;
        }
        // A page that big, with its header, has no int size.
        if size > LARGEST - size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page) { return null; }
        bytes := TEMPORARY_STORAGE_SIZE;
        if size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page) + size > bytes { bytes = size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page) + size; }
        overflow := storage.overflow_allocator;
        fresh := cast(*Temporary_Storage_Page) overflow.proc(ALLOCATE, bytes, 0, null, overflow.data);
        if fresh == null { return null; }
        fresh.previous = page;
        fresh.size = bytes - size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page);
        // A region whose size isn't a multiple of 8, a large page's, would
        // otherwise start the page at a count that isn't one. The gap
        // counts as occupied, as the tail does.
        fresh.start = (start + region_size + 7) & ~7;
        storage.overflow_pages = fresh;
        storage.occupied = fresh.start + size;
        if storage.occupied > storage.high_water_mark { storage.high_water_mark = storage.occupied; }
        return cast(*u8) fresh + size_of(Temporary_Storage_Page);
    }
    if mode == RESIZE {
        // The last allocation ends where the region's offset is. old_size
        // within the offset keeps old_memory in the region, and its own
        // offset is then the region's less old_size.
        if old_memory != null && old_size <= offset && cast(*u8) old_memory + old_size == base + offset {
            from := offset - old_size;
            if size <= region_size - from {
                storage.occupied = start + from + size;
                if storage.occupied > storage.high_water_mark { storage.high_water_mark = storage.occupied; }
                return old_memory;
            }
        }
        memory := temporary_allocator_proc(ALLOCATE, size, 0, null, data);
        if memory == null { return null; }
        count := old_size;
        if size < count { count = size; }
        // A count of 0 or less copies nothing, and never reaches C as a
        // size_t that wrapped.
        if count > 0 { __memmove(memory, old_memory, cast(u64) count); }
        return memory;
    }
    if mode == FREE_ALL {
        overflow := storage.overflow_allocator;
        while page != null {
            previous := page.previous;
            overflow.proc(FREE, 0, 0, page, overflow.data);
            page = previous;
        }
        storage.overflow_pages = null;
        storage.occupied = 0;
    }
    return null;
}

// The temporary storage as an Allocator. Its procedure finds the storage
// through the context, so it needs no data.
TEMPORARY_ALLOCATOR :: Allocator.{temporary_allocator_proc, null};

// Resizable arrays (spec 3.17): [..]T, Jai's Resizable_Array. Its layout,
// which the compiler mirrors (src/types.h's RESIZABLE_ALLOCATED_OFFSET,
// RESIZABLE_ALLOCATOR_OFFSET and RESIZABLE_SIZE): a view's count and data,
// where a view has them, so whatever reads a view's reads a resizable
// array's, then allocated, how many elements data's memory holds, and the
// allocator that memory came from and goes back to. The array owns that
// memory when allocated is above 0 and its elements take bytes; at 0 it
// owns none, though a program may have set data and count by hand, and an
// array of elements of 0 bytes owns none either (__array_no_bytes). This
// is the untyped form, data a *void, which every [..]T has whatever its T,
// so code that works on any of them takes one; the compiler needs it for a
// [..]T at all, its allocator field being this one's.
Resizable_Array :: struct {
    count: int;
    data: *void;
    allocated: int;
    allocator: Allocator;
}

// What the resizable arrays' built-in procedures call (spec 3.17):
// array_add calls __array_add, array_insert __array_insert, and so on for
// the nine. They're written here, in Rig, so the growth policy and the
// allocator rule are the same compiled and interpreted, and while
// compiling. Each takes the untyped array, which every [..]T is laid out
// as, and its element's size in bytes, which only the caller's T says;
// lowering passes the size as a constant, add's and insert's item by the
// address of a copy of its own, so an item that is one of the array's own
// elements is read before the array grows or its elements move, and pop
// the address its last element is copied to, the built-in's result.
// Lowering checks the pointer and resize's count first, at the built-in's
// name, and the index and pop's count too unless bounds checks are off
// (#no_abc, --no-bounds-checks; spec 7.7), when an index outside the array
// is a memory error no check catches (spec 8.6). For every built-in but
// array_reset and array_free, which use no element, it checks too, bounds
// checks on or off, what the helpers trust the array to say of itself: a
// count of 0 or more (but in array_reserve, which places no element from
// it); a data that isn't null when the count or allocated is above 0,
// since that count says elements are there and that allocated says room
// is, which no memory holds, so the built-in stops as indexing would; and
// in array_pop and the removes, a count not past allocated when the array
// owns memory, since they read and move its last elements. The helpers
// check none of them, and a program that calls one by hand gets no check.
//
// The allocator: an array whose allocator's procedure is null, a zeroed
// one, takes the caller's context.allocator when it first grows, and keeps
// it, even when it gives no memory, so it grows and goes back to that
// allocator wherever it is used later. A program that sets .allocator
// before the first growth chooses another; changing it while the array
// holds memory is a memory error no check catches (spec 8.6).
//
// Owning memory: the array owns data's memory when allocated is above 0
// and its elements take bytes, and gives it back with FREE, its old_size
// allocated elements' bytes, so an allocator that records sizes gets them.
// An array whose data and count a program set by hand, allocated 0, owns
// none: its first growth copies its elements into memory of its own,
// leaving theirs alone, and array_free gives nothing back for it. Elements
// of 0 bytes take no memory at all, so an array of them owns none, whatever
// its allocated (__array_no_bytes).
//
// The place: the five helpers that can reach the allocator, __array_add,
// __array_insert, __array_reserve, __array_resize and __array_free, take
// loc last, the place of the built-in's name in its call, which lowering
// makes as it makes a default #caller_location (spec 7.2). __array_grow,
// which the first four reach the allocator through, and __array_free
// put it in context.allocation_location while they call the allocator,
// and put the one before back after, as Basic's alloc and free do (spec
// 7.10), so an allocator that records where it is asked from names the
// program's line, not Preload's. It has no default: lowering always
// passes it, and a program that calls a helper by hand passes one too,
// .{} for no place.

// C's memset, under Preload's own name, as __memmove is: __array_resize
// zeroes the elements it adds with it.
__memset :: (destination: *void, value: s32, count: u64) -> *void #foreign libc "memset";

// Where an array of elements of 0 bytes ([..]Empty, for a struct Empty
// with no fields) points once it has grown: they take no memory, but an
// array holding some isn't null, so indexing and for find its data, and
// every element's address is this one's. Nothing reads or writes it. It's
// an int, not a byte, for its alignment: an element of 0 bytes can still
// need 8-byte alignment, as a struct holding a [0]int does, and its
// address must have it (spec 3.11).
__array_no_bytes : int;

// Makes room in array for room elements of size bytes, or for its count
// when that is more, and says whether the allocator gave the memory. On
// false the array is as it was, but for the allocator it took.
//
// An array that owns no memory (allocated 0, or data null) is given new
// memory with ALLOCATE, and its count elements at data, unless data is
// null, are copied there; one that owns some has it resized with RESIZE,
// from its allocated elements' bytes. Room whose bytes pass int's largest
// is more than any memory holds: it asks for int's largest bytes, which
// no allocator gives. Elements of 0 bytes ask for nothing.
//
// While the allocator is called, context.allocation_location is loc, the
// built-in's place its caller passed on. The place before is put back by
// a defer, when the procedure returns, so after it the context is as it
// was, whether the allocator gave memory or not.
__array_grow :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, room: int, loc: Source_Code_Location) -> bool {
    LARGEST :: 9223372036854775807; // int's largest value
    if array.allocator.proc == null { array.allocator = context.allocator; }
    elements := room;
    if elements < array.count { elements = array.count; }
    if size <= 0 {
        array.data = *__array_no_bytes;
        array.allocated = elements;
        return true;
    }
    bytes := LARGEST;
    if elements <= LARGEST / size { bytes = elements * size; }
    allocator := array.allocator;
    saved := context.allocation_location;
    context.allocation_location = loc;
    defer context.allocation_location = saved;
    memory : *void;
    if array.allocated <= 0 || array.data == null {
        memory = allocator.proc(ALLOCATE, bytes, 0, null, allocator.data);
        if memory == null { return false; }
        // Within bytes, since elements is at least the count. A count of 0
        // or less copies nothing, and never reaches C as a size_t that
        // wrapped.
        if array.data != null && array.count > 0 {
            __memmove(memory, array.data, cast(u64) (array.count * size));
        }
    } else {
        memory = allocator.proc(RESIZE, bytes, array.allocated * size, array.data, allocator.data);
        if memory == null { return false; }
    }
    array.data = memory;
    array.allocated = elements;
    return true;
}

// array_add: a copy of the size bytes at item as the new last element.
// False when the array was full and its allocator had no memory to give,
// or its count is int's largest (__array_insert): the array is as it was,
// but for the allocator it took.
__array_add :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, item: *void, loc: Source_Code_Location) -> bool {
    return __array_insert(array, size, array.count, item, loc);
}

// array_insert: a copy of the size bytes at item as element index, from 0
// to the count, the elements from index on moving up one. False when the
// array was full and its allocator had no memory to give, or its count is
// int's largest (below): the array is as it was, but for the allocator it
// took.
//
// A full array, its count at least allocated, grows to the larger of 8
// and twice allocated: doubling, rather than growing by one, means n
// elements added one at a time are moved about n times in all, not about
// n * n / 2. Doubling past half of int's largest would wrap, so room past
// it is int's largest. The room is never below the count plus one, since
// an array whose data and count were set by hand may count more than its
// allocated 0; a count of int's largest has no room for one more.
__array_insert :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, index: int, item: *void, loc: Source_Code_Location) -> bool {
    LARGEST :: 9223372036854775807; // int's largest value
    HALF :: LARGEST / 2;
    if array.count >= array.allocated {
        room := LARGEST;
        if array.allocated <= HALF { room = array.allocated * 2; }
        if room < 8 { room = 8; }
        if room <= array.count {
            if array.count == LARGEST { return false; }
            room = array.count + 1;
        }
        if !__array_grow(array, size, room, loc) { return false; }
    }
    at := cast(*u8) array.data + index * size;
    moved := (array.count - index) * size;
    if moved > 0 { __memmove(at + size, at, cast(u64) moved); }
    if size > 0 { __memmove(at, item, cast(u64) size); }
    array.count += 1;
    return true;
}

// array_pop: copies the last element into the size bytes at item, and
// removes it. The array has one: lowering checks, unless bounds checks are
// off.
__array_pop :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, item: *void) {
    array.count -= 1;
    if size > 0 { __memmove(item, cast(*u8) array.data + array.count * size, cast(u64) size); }
}

// array_remove_ordered: removes element index, moving the later elements
// down one, so they keep their order.
__array_remove_ordered :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, index: int) {
    at := cast(*u8) array.data + index * size;
    moved := (array.count - 1 - index) * size;
    if moved > 0 { __memmove(at, at + size, cast(u64) moved); }
    array.count -= 1;
}

// array_remove_unordered: removes element index by moving the last element
// into its place, which copies one element rather than all the later ones,
// and changes their order.
__array_remove_unordered :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, index: int) {
    array.count -= 1;
    if index != array.count && size > 0 {
        data := cast(*u8) array.data;
        __memmove(data + index * size, data + array.count * size, cast(u64) size);
    }
}

// array_reserve: makes allocated at least count, growing to exactly count
// when it is more, or to the array's count when that is more still, as for
// an array whose data and count were set by hand (__array_grow), so that
// many elements are added without growing again. A count within
// allocated, or below 0, does nothing. False when the allocator had no
// memory to give: the array is as it was, but for the allocator it took.
__array_reserve :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, count: int, loc: Source_Code_Location) -> bool {
    if count <= array.allocated { return true; }
    return __array_grow(array, size, count, loc);
}

// array_resize: makes count the array's count, growing to exactly count
// when it's past allocated, or to the array's count when that is more, as
// for an array whose data and count were set by hand (__array_grow). The
// elements it adds are zeroed; shrinking keeps the memory. The count isn't
// below 0: lowering checks. False when the allocator had no memory to
// give: the array is as it was, but for the allocator it took.
__array_resize :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, count: int, loc: Source_Code_Location) -> bool {
    if count > array.allocated {
        if !__array_grow(array, size, count, loc) { return false; }
    }
    added := (count - array.count) * size;
    if added > 0 { __memset(cast(*u8) array.data + array.count * size, 0, cast(u64) added); }
    array.count = count;
    return true;
}

// array_reset: empties the array for reuse, its count 0 and its memory
// kept, so as many elements are added again without growing. It doesn't
// use size, which every helper takes.
__array_reset :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int) {
    array.count = 0;
}

// array_free: gives the memory the array owns back to its allocator, with
// FREE and its size. The array is then empty, data null and allocated 0,
// and keeps its allocator, so it can grow again from it. While the
// allocator is called, context.allocation_location is loc, and the place
// before is put back after, as in __array_grow.
__array_free :: (array: *Resizable_Array, size: int, loc: Source_Code_Location) {
    if array.allocated > 0 && array.data != null && size > 0 {
        allocator := array.allocator;
        saved := context.allocation_location;
        context.allocation_location = loc;
        defer context.allocation_location = saved;
        allocator.proc(FREE, 0, array.allocated * size, array.data, allocator.data);
    }
    array.count = 0;
    array.data = null;
    array.allocated = 0;
}

// A place in the program's source, Jai's: what a parameter's default value
// #caller_location is (spec 7.2), the place of each call that leaves the
// parameter out, `loc: Source_Code_Location = #caller_location`. file is
// the path messages name the call's file by, as a runtime error there would
// (spec 8.1): the main file's as rig build was given it, a loaded file's
// as its #load made it, a module's file by its absolute path. line and
// column are those of the callee's name in the call, counted from 1 (spec
// 2.2). A zeroed one, file empty and line 0, is no place. The context holds
// one, the place an allocation is asked from (Context, above).
Source_Code_Location :: struct { file: string; line: int; column: int; }
